WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:03.989
No rules this time, just a little history.

00:00:03.989 --> 00:00:06.040
Alexander Hamilton was many things:

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:10.170
aid to General George Washington, Secretary
of the Treasury, Major General of the US Army,

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.910
lover, cheater, duelist, musical phenomenon.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:17.349
But few knew his immensely influential role
in American Free Press Law

00:00:17.349 --> 00:00:19.740
just months before his fatal duel.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.300
Today we think of Libel as defamatory falsehood.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:25.820
False written statements, especially lies
but sometimes honest mistakes,

00:00:25.829 --> 00:00:27.980
that injure a person's reputation.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:32.829
And we also think of libel as a civil claim,
criminal libel prosecutions are very rare.

00:00:32.829 --> 00:00:36.040
In 1700s England though, criminal libel cases
were common

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:39.760
and they covered many written statements that
harmed a person's reputation

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:41.240
even if they were true.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:45.640
Such statements were outlawed in part because
they were seen as likely to produce duels.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:50.140
Hamilton died because of his harsh statements,
albeit oral statements, about Aaron Burr.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:55.140
And when said of government officials, such
defamatory statements, again even if true,

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.040
were seen as undermining the government's
authority.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.200
'The greater the truth, the greater the libel.'

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.080
some said.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.840
American law was based on English law,

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:06.660
so many Americans assumed American law would
take the same view.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:10.840
In the famous colonial era 1735 John Peter
Zenger trial,

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.520
the defense had argued that truth must be
a defense in libel cases

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:19.570
but though the jury acquitted Zenger, such
jury decisions set no legal finding precedent.

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:22.630
Enter Alexander Hamilton in 1803.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:26.149
Thomas Jefferson was President, Hamilton was
a prominent New York lawyer.

00:01:26.149 --> 00:01:29.130
When Harry Croswell, an anti-Jefferson newspaper
editor,

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:32.819
was prosecuted in New York State Court for
libeling Jefferson

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.100
Hamilton came to Croswell's defense.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:40.100
Croswell's publication had alleged that Thomas
Jefferson had payed another editor, James Callender,

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:43.360
to make scurrilous against Washington and
Adams.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:48.160
This allegation of Croswell's injured Jefferson's
reputation, the prosecution charged,

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:51.580
thus making it libel without regard to whether
it was true.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:55.440
And it also injured the nation, making it
so-called seditious libel.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:56.680
Croswell was convicted

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:01.460
after the trial judge instructed the jury that truth was not a defense in libel cases.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.639
Croswell appealed and Hamilton, representing
Croswell,

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:07.260
argued that truth should have been a defense.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:33.950
Today that standard actually would diminish
First Amendment protection.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:37.880
At least as to matters of public concern,
the court held in 1964,

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:41.000
prosecutors must always prove that an alleged
libel was false

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:45.530
regardless of whether it was said from good
motives and for justifiable ends.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:50.280
But in 1803 Hamilton's position was a great
step towards broader legal protection

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:51.810
for criticism of government.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:54.069
And Hamilton's position swept the nation.

00:02:54.069 --> 00:02:56.510
Not at first, the New York court split 2-2

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:58.689
thus leaving Croswell's conviction standing.

00:02:58.689 --> 00:02:59.690
But Justice James Kent,

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:04.500
who had become one of the most influential
judges and legal writers of the early 1800s,

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.360
endorsed Hamilton's view in his opinion.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:10.840
In 1805 the New York Legislature enacted a
statute implementing Hamilton's view

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:16.290
that truth was always a defense when published
with good motives and for justifiable ends,

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.319
phrasing that Hamilton pioneered.

00:03:18.319 --> 00:03:22.840
In the decades after that, many state constitutions
were framed precisely this way.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.780
To this day 20 state constitutions contain
Hamilton's formula.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:30.940
Hamilton and Justice Kent had become close
friends in the years before the Croswell case.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:34.360
When they were in Albany for the court sitting
that included the Croswell argument,

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:37.000
Hamilton, Kent and a few others had dinner
together.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:41.360
Over dinner Hamilton remarked that he thought
Aaron Burr was dangerous and untrustworthy.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.270
Burr was at the time planning to run for Governor
of New York

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:48.200
though he ended up being beaten by Morgan
Lewis, the trial judge in Croswell's case.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.980
Another man at the dinner reported on these
remarks

00:03:50.989 --> 00:03:53.489
which were then referred to in an Albany newspaper.

00:03:53.489 --> 00:03:58.230
Burr demanded that they be retracted, Hamilton
refused, Burr challenged Hamilton to a duel

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:03.420
and Hamilton didn't live to see his view of
the freedom of the press become part of American law.

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:14.560
I'm Eugene Volokh and I approve this message.

